{
  "families": [
    {
      "budget_share": 0.55,
      "family": "pipelined",
      "ranges": [
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 22,
          "low": 12,
          "param": "data_width"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 14,
          "low": 10,
          "param": "n_iter"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 2,
          "low": -2,
          "param": "angle_guard"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 2,
          "low": 0,
          "param": "frac_guard"
        }
      ],
      "why": "Fully pipelined gives 1 result/cycle, so throughput = Fmax. To hit >=1 MSPS we need Fmax >= 1 MHz, which is trivially met, so the binding constraint is max_abs_err <= 2^-10. Accuracy is driven by n_iter and the guard bits; data_width sets the output LSB (2^-(W-2)) so W must be large enough that the truncation floor plus CORDIC residual stays under 2^-10. I focus n_iter in 10..14 (enough to push residual below 2^-10 while keeping area low) and keep data_width in 12..22 to capture the area/accuracy trade-off. angle_guard -2..2 and frac_guard 0..2 cover the accuracy levers without wasting area on over-guarding. Both rounding modes included since rounding can shave error at negligible area cost."
    },
    {
      "budget_share": 0.45,
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 22,
          "low": 12,
          "param": "data_width"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 14,
          "low": 10,
          "param": "n_iter"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 2,
          "low": -2,
          "param": "angle_guard"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 2,
          "low": 0,
          "param": "frac_guard"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 4,
          "low": 2,
          "param": "m"
        }
      ],
      "why": "pipelined_m registers every m stages, so it uses fewer flip-flops than fully pipelined at the same N, directly reducing area (the primary objective) while still delivering 1 result/cycle (throughput = Fmax, constraint easily met). m in 2..4 trades latency (ceil(N/m)+2) against register count; since latency is not a spec constraint, larger m is favored for area. Same data_width/n_iter/guard/rounding ranges as pipelined so the two fronts are directly comparable and the merged Pareto front spans the register-density trade-off. This family is where the minimum-area corner is most likely to live."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec is area-dominated with a hard throughput floor (>=1 MSPS) and a hard accuracy floor (max_abs_err <= 2^-10). Both pipelined and pipelined_m produce one result per cycle, so throughput equals Fmax; the 1 MSPS floor is non-binding (Fmax will be far above 1 MHz), leaving area and accuracy as the real trade-off. Accuracy is set by n_iter plus the guard bits and the output LSB (2^-(W-2)), independent of the pipeline structure, so I use identical data_width/n_iter/angle_guard/frac_guard/rounding ranges across both families to make their fronts comparable. The key differentiator is register count: pipelined_m with m>1 uses fewer FFs than pipelined at equal N, so it should host the minimum-area designs, while pipelined (m=1) gives the lowest latency and a clean reference point on the front. I keep n_iter in 10..14 (enough to clear 2^-10 without over-designing) and data_width in 12..22 to map the area-vs-accuracy curve. I split the budget 55/45 toward pipelined to first confirm the accuracy-feasible region and establish the reference front, with pipelined_m carrying the area-minimization search. If the first round shows the accuracy constraint is tight or the area floor is high, I will tighten n_iter/data_width and push more budget into pipelined_m with larger m in the next round."
}
